{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794861860372,"sessionID":"ses_e19ec4786ffeqnGAoXEPfPmRLE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_3373b3355e194d37a40dd5ce","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Guards against drifting away from the ``moneyfmt`` 2.0.0 API.\"\"\"\n\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport unittest\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport invoicer  # noqa: F401  (puts the vendored moneyfmt on sys.path)\nimport moneyfmt\nfrom invoicer.totals import Line, parse_line, render, total\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\nclass VendoredMoneyfmtTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_vendored_version_is_2_0_0(self):\n        self.assertEqual(moneyfmt.__version__, \"2.0.0\")\n\n    def test_error_class_was_renamed(self):\n        self.assertTrue(issubclass(moneyfmt.MoneyFormatError, ValueError))\n        self.assertFalse(hasattr(moneyfmt, \"MoneyError\"))\n\n    def test_format_money_takes_a_decimal_and_a_keyword_currency(self):\n        self.assertEqual(moneyfmt.format_money(Decimal(\"1234.5\"), currency=\"EUR\"), \"€1,234.50\")\n        with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n            moneyfmt.format_money(1234.5, currency=\"EUR\")\n        with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n            moneyfmt.format_money(Decimal(\"1\"), \"EUR\")\n\n    def test_parse_money_returns_a_decimal(self):\n        self.assertIsInstance(moneyfmt.parse_money(\"1,234.50\"), Decimal)\n\n\nclass ExactDecimalAmountsTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_amounts_are_decimals(self):\n        line = parse_line(\"Design=1,200.00\")\n        self.assertIsInstance(line.amount, Decimal)\n        self.assertEqual(line.amount, Decimal(\"1200.00\"))\n\n    def test_total_is_exact(self):\n        lines = [parse_line(\"A=0.10\"), parse_line(\"B=0.20\")]\n        self.assertEqual(total(lines), Decimal(\"0.30\"))\n        # a float total would carry the 0.1 + 0.2 rounding error\n        self.assertNotEqual(total(lines), 0.1 + 0.2)\n\n    def test_rendered_total_is_exact(self):\n        rows = render([parse_line(\"A=0.10\"), parse_line(\"B=0.20\")])\n        self.assertEqual(rows[-1], \"Total                            €0.30\")\n\n    def test_line_keeps_plain_numbers_decimal(self):\n        self.assertEqual(Line(\"Design\", 10).amount, Decimal(\"10\"))\n\n\nclass CommandLineTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def run_cli(self, *args):\n        return subprocess.run(\n            [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"invoicer\", *args],\n            capture_output=True,\n            text=True,\n            cwd=ROOT,\n        )\n\n    def test_invoice_is_printed(self):\n        result = self.run_cli(\"Design=1,200.00\", \"Hosting=49.99\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 0)\n        self.assertEqual(\n            result.stdout.splitlines(),\n            [\n                \"Design                       €1,200.00\",\n                \"Hosting                         €49.99\",\n                \"Total                        €1,249.99\",\n            ],\n        )\n\n    def test_bad_amount_reports_the_error_and_exits_2(self):\n        result = self.run_cli(\"Design=lots\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 2)\n        self.assertEqual(result.stdout, \"\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.stderr.strip(), \"error: cannot read amount 'lots'\")\n\n    def test_without_arguments_the_usage_is_printed_and_it_exits_2(self):\n        result = self.run_cli()\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 2)\n        self.assertIn(\"usage: python -m invoicer\", result.stderr)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n","filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/dependency-upgrade/opencode-1/repo/tests/test_moneyfmt_api.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/dependency-upgrade/opencode-1/repo/tests/test_moneyfmt_api.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/test_moneyfmt_api.py","time":{"start":1794861860339,"end":1794861860363}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait: there's an issue with `Line.__post_init__` coercion: `Decimal(str(amount))` for a float like 1.005 gives Decimal(\"1.005\") — good.\n\nBut hold on: `total()` sums `line.amount` — if a Line was constructed with a Decimal, fine. The coercion guarantees Decimal always.\n\nNow, one concern with the coercion: the old behavior for `Line(\"d\", 1.005)` was float 1.005 → format_money → Decimal(str(1.005)) = 1.005 → quantize HALF_UP → 1.01. New: Decimal(\"1.005\") → 1.01. Same.\n\nFor a float like 0.1 + 0.2 artifact passed directly as Line amount: old: str(0.30000000000000004) → 0.30. New: same coercion → same.\n\nNow the CLI: `parse_line` uses parse_money which returns Decimal → no coercion needed.\n\nNow the tests. Let me also double-check the `render` line length / formatting - the repo might have a linter (ruff?) but there's no config. Let me check for config files: pyproject.toml, setup.cfg, .ruff.toml — none found earlier (only README, requirements, invoicer, tests, vendor). So no lint config. Still, keep style tidy.\n\nNow write the new test file. Tests should:\n1. Verify the vendored moneyfmt is 2.0.0 with the new API (MoneyFormatError exists, MoneyError gone, format_money keyword-only currency, Decimal-only amount).\n2. Verify the invoicer produces exact decimals (no float artifacts) — catches a missed adaptation (e.g. float() conversions).\n3. Verify the CLI error path: exit status 2 and the same message (catches a missed exception rename → traceback/exit 1).\n\nLet me write tests/test_moneyfmt_api.py.\n\nFor the CLI subprocess test, I need the repo root on sys.path for `python -m invoicer`. When running `python -m unittest discover -s tests` from the repo root, cwd is repo root → `python -m invoicer` works if cwd is in sys.path for the subprocess (subprocess inherits cwd; python -m adds cwd to sys.path). I'll compute the repo root from __file__ and pass cwd=repo_root to be robust.\n\nTest details:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport unittest\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport moneyfmt\nfrom invoicer.totals import InvoiceError, Line, parse_line, render, total\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\nclass VendoredMoneyfmtTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_vendored_version_is_2_0_0(self):\n        self.assertEqual(moneyfmt.__version__, \"2.0.0\")\n\n    def test_error_class_was_renamed(self):\n        self.assertTrue(issubclass(moneyfmt.MoneyFormatError, ValueError))\n        self.assertFalse(hasattr(moneyfmt, \"MoneyError\"))\n\n    def test_format_money_takes_a_decimal_and_keyword_currency(self):\n        self.assertEqual(moneyfmt.format_money(Decimal(\"1234.5\"), currency=\"EUR\"), \"€1,234.50\")\n        with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n            moneyfmt.format_money(1234.5, currency=\"EUR\")\n        with self.assertRaises(TypeError):\n            moneyfmt.format_money(Decimal(\"1\"), \"EUR\")\n\n\nclass ExactDecimalAmountsTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_amounts_are_decimals(self):\n        line = parse_line(\"Design=1,200.00\")\n        self.assertIsInstance(line.amount, Decimal)\n        self.assertEqual(line.amount, Decimal(\"1200.00\"))\n\n    def test_total_is_exact(self):\n        lines = [parse_line(\"A=0.10\"), parse_line(\"B=0.20\")]\n        self.assertEqual(total(lines), Decimal(\"0.30\"))\n        # a float-based total would carry the 0.1 + 0.2 rounding error\n        self.assertNotEqual(total(lines), 0.1 + 0.2)\n\n    def test_render_total_is_exact(self):\n        rows = render([parse_line(\"A=0.10\"), parse_line(\"B=0.20\")])\n        self.assertEqual(rows[-1], \"Total                            €0.30\")\n\n    def test_line_accepts_plain_numbers(self):\n        self.assertEqual(Line(\"Design\", 10).amount, Decimal(\"10\"))\n```\n\nHmm, `assertNotEqual(total(lines), 0.1 + 0.2)`: Decimal(\"0.30\") == 0.30000000000000004 → False → assertNotEqual passes. Good. But note Decimal.__eq__ with float works in Python 3. Yes, Decimal supports comparison with float.\n\nAlso a CLI test:\n\n```python\nclass CommandLineTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def run_cli(self, *args):\n        return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"invoicer\", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=ROOT)\n\n    def test_error_exit_status_and_message(self):\n        result = self.run_cli(\"Design=lots\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 2)\n        self.assertEqual(result.stderr.strip(), \"error: cannot read amount 'lots'\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.stdout, \"\")\n\n    def test_usage_without_arguments(self):\n        result = self.run_cli()\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 2)\n        self.assertIn(\"usage:\", result.stderr)\n\n    def test_invoice_output(self):\n        result = self.run_cli(\"Design=1,200.00\", \"Hosting=49.99\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.returncode, 0)\n        self.assertEqual(result.stdout.splitlines(), [...])\n```\n\nNote: the CLI test relies on the vendored moneyfmt being importable in the subprocess — cwd=ROOT means `python -m invoicer` works since invoicer/__init__.py inserts the vendor path. Good.\n\nWait — one potential issue: `import moneyfmt` in the test file. The test file imports `invoicer.totals` which imports the `invoicer` package first → `invoicer/__init__.py` adds vendor to sys.path. But the import order in the test module: `import moneyfmt` comes before `from invoicer.totals import ...` alphabetically in my file. Python executes imports in order, so `import moneyfmt` would run BEFORE invoicer is imported → ImportError unless moneyfmt is installed in site-packages. Danger! I must import invoicer first, or rely on the fact that `invoicer` package import happens... no, order matters.\n\nSafest: `import invoicer  # noqa` first, or do `from invoicer.totals import ...` before `import moneyfmt` — but linters/isort would reorder. Alternative: `from invoicer import ...`? Let me structure:\n\n```python\nfrom invoicer.totals import InvoiceError, Line, parse_line, render, total  # noqa: F401  (adds vendor/ to sys.path)\n\nimport moneyfmt\n```\n\nisort would put `import moneyfmt` first (stdlib/third-party grouping). Hmm. Instead, I can do:\n\n```python\nimport invoicer  # noqa: F401  # puts the vendored moneyfmt on sys.path\nimport moneyfmt\n```\n\nAlphabetically \"invoicer\" < \"moneyfmt\" → order fine, and `import invoicer` executes the package __init__ which sets sys.path. Then `import moneyfmt` works. \n\nBut careful: is `invoicer` importable when running `python -m unittest discover -s tests`? unittest discover with -s tests inserts the top-level directory (tests' parent? ) — discover sets top_level_dir to the start dir by default, which is `tests`. Hmm! `python -m unittest discover -s tests` — the top level directory defaults to the start directory (tests). Then it inserts `tests` into sys.path, and test modules are imported as `test_totals` etc. But `tests/__init__.py` exists, so discovery treats `tests` as a package and sets top_level_dir to the parent (repo root). Indeed, since tests/ has __init__.py, unittest walks up to find the top-level directory → repo root is inserted into sys.path. So `import invoicer` works. The existing test file already does `from invoicer.totals import ...` and it works. Good.\n\nBut also `import moneyfmt` — after `import invoicer`, sys.path includes vendor → moneyfmt importable. \n\nAlso note: in the subprocess CLI test, cwd=ROOT ensures `python -m invoicer` resolves invoicer package from repo root. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: should the new test file be named test_moneyfmt_api.py? Fine.\n\nLet me also double check `render` output width: \"Total\" left-justified in 24 chars + amount right-justified in 14 → \"Total\" + 19 spaces + \"€0.30\" (5 chars) → 9 spaces padding. Let me compute: f\"{'Total':<24}\" = \"Total\" + 19 spaces = 24 chars. f\"{'€0.30':>14}\" = 9 spaces + \"€0.30\" = 14 chars. Total string: \"Total\" + 19 spaces + 9 spaces + \"€0.30\" = \"Total\" + 28 spaces + \"€0.30\". In the old output for \"A=0.10\": \"Total                            €0.30\" — that's \"Total\" + 28 spaces + \"€0.30\". ✓ (matches my expected literal? Let me count in my test string: \"Total                            €0.30\" — I copied from the battery output: \"Total                            \\u20ac0.30\" — 28 spaces. Let me count the spaces in the battery JSON: \"Total\" then spaces... I'll just verify by running the test.)\n\nAlso the euro sign width: Python's str formatting counts \"€\" as 1 char. ✓\n\nNow let me write the test file and run everything.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e61a8548001yMfSR7ruLjEbr2","sessionID":"ses_e19ec4786ffeqnGAoXEPfPmRLE","messageID":"msg_1e619b4c5001PhPyL4l9nrwwkS"}}
