{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794861953165,"sessionID":"ses_e19ec4786ffeqnGAoXEPfPmRLE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_f553171f5a644017af682067","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cmp vendor/moneyfmt/__init__.py vendor-updates/moneyfmt-2.0.0/moneyfmt/__init__.py && echo \"vendor file byte-identical to the 2.0.0 release\"; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -4; echo \"--- README example ---\"; python3 -m invoicer \"Design=1,200.00\" \"Hosting=49.99\"; echo \"exit=$?\""},"output":"vendor file byte-identical to the 2.0.0 release\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 13 tests in 0.131s\n\nOK\n--- README example ---\nDesign                       €1,200.00\nHosting                         €49.99\nTotal                        €1,249.99\nexit=0\n","metadata":{"output":"vendor file byte-identical to the 2.0.0 release\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 13 tests in 0.131s\n\nOK\n--- README example ---\nDesign                       €1,200.00\nHosting                         €49.99\nTotal                        €1,249.99\nexit=0\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cmp vendor/moneyfmt/__init__.py vendor-updates/moneyfmt-2.0.0/moneyfmt/__init__.py && echo \"vendor file byte-identical to the 2.0.0 release\"; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -4; echo \"--- README example ---\"; python3 -m invoicer \"Design=1,200.00\" \"Hosting=49.99\"; echo \"exit=$?\"","time":{"start":1794861952867,"end":1794861953159}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Everything looks good. The vendor diff shows only the library's own changes (file copied verbatim).\n\nOne more consideration: the `Line.__post_init__` coercion. Let me double-check the interplay with `parse_money` returning Decimal → isinstance check passes → no coercion. Good.\n\nAlso, `total()` with a mix of Decimals → fine.\n\nLet me reconsider whether the coercion is desirable, since it might be seen as \"re-introducing float support\". The alternative is that `Line(\"d\", 1.5)` raises TypeError when rendered — a regression for anyone constructing lines directly (the old API allowed floats). I'll keep the coercion but make sure the comment is clear. Actually, let me reconsider the comment wording: \"``moneyfmt`` 2.0.0 formats ``Decimal`` only, so keep amounts decimal here.\" Good.\n\nHmm, one thing: `Decimal(str(self.amount))` for a float nan/inf → Decimal(\"nan\") → then format_money would raise InvalidOperation (uncaught). Old behavior with float nan: `Decimal(str(nan))` = Decimal('NaN') → quantize → InvalidOperation → traceback. Same as before (uncaught). Edge case, not important; but should `Line` reject NaN? parse_money already rejects NaN/Infinity now, so lines built via parse_line can't have them. Direct construction with nan is an edge case — old code also crashed. Fine.\n\nNow let me also double check python 3.11 compatibility (README says Python 3.11+): `dataclass(frozen=True)` with `__post_init__` and object.__setattr__ works in 3.11. `sum(..., Decimal(\"0\"))` fine. Type hints with `from __future__ import annotations` fine. `Decimal.is_finite` fine.\n\nAlso check: `format_money(Decimal(\"1234.5\"), currency=\"EUR\")` → \"€1,234.50\" ✓ (test passes).\n\nLet me run the full test suite one final time plus the README example, and verify the vendored file is byte-identical to the release (already diffed). Also verify that the file was copied without any modification — `cmp` confirms.\n\nLet me also run a final check that `python -m unittest discover -s tests` passes from a different cwd? The README says run from repo root. Fine.\n\nOne more: let's confirm the vendored file is byte-identical with cmp and check file permissions/mode (copied with cp → mode preserved? The original in vendor-updates was 644; cp overwrote the file content keeping the existing file's mode). Let me check: `ls -l` showed -rw-r--r-- for both. Good.\n\nFinal verification run.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e61bef120017jMkMVJWUYcT1U","sessionID":"ses_e19ec4786ffeqnGAoXEPfPmRLE","messageID":"msg_1e61b81f4001s624W97qU5szDt"}}
